  stand down he said that the solemn  islands is paralyzed and can't afford  another round of instability but the  prime minister he made an emotional  often fiery defense calling some of his  critics  thugs and hooligans but as i mentioned  he survived yet another motion of no  confidence and will continue his  government will continue  leading the country what does this mean  for concern about continued street  unrest though  well certainly there was some uh concern  that uh on monday with this vote of no  confidence being debated in parliament  that there could be some uh unrest in  the aftermath no matter which way it  went that we had businesses i was shut  down or closed early we had a huge and  heavy police presence across honiara and  also as you mentioned earlier we've got  the peacekeepers who've been  enforced over the last couple of weeks  since those four days of civil unrest  where we saw i had three people died as  a result those peacekeepers are there  but these are long-running grievances  between the state i mentioned the  province the malaysian province uh and  the central government accusations of  neglect that infrastructure had not been  would have been abandoned and economic  funds had been frozen and these these  particular accusations are not going to  go away and there's also the criticism  of the solomon islands ditching its  partnership with taiwan in favor of  china but as i mentioned that the prime  minister has denied all these  allegations he said the country is in in  goods a good  position at the moment he's also denied  allegations of corruption uh but we  spoke to an analyst who looks at the  pacific and he pretty much said that he  doesn't believe or there could  there could be some unrest in the near  future but definitely down the track  these grievances will continue uh to be  a problem in the solomon islands  all right thanks so much  sarah clark there
 

